A concise, valid Graphviz schematic could show the same candidate hyperparameter settings evaluated across each fold, followed by aggregation and selection:

```dot
digraph kfold_tuning {
  rankdir=LR;
  graph [bgcolor="#FFFDF7", fontname="Arial", nodesep=0.35, ranksep=0.6];
  node [shape=box, style="rounded,filled", fontname="Arial", color="#455A64"];
  edge [color="#607D8B", arrowsize=0.8];

  data   [label="Training dataset", fillcolor="#FFF3B0"];
  params [label="Candidate hyperparameters\n(setting 1, 2, ...)", fillcolor="#C8E6C9"];
  scores [label="Fold scores\n(one score per fold)", fillcolor="#D7E8FA"];
  mean   [label="Aggregate scores\n(e.g., mean ± std. dev.)", fillcolor="#F8C9D4"];
  select [label="Select best\nhyperparameters", shape=diamond, fillcolor="#E1C8F2"];
  refit  [label="Refit on all training data;\nevaluate on held-out test set", fillcolor="#BFE8DF"];

  data -> params [label="evaluate each candidate"];
  params -> scores;
  scores -> mean;
  mean -> select;
  select -> refit;

  subgraph cluster_folds {
    label="K folds: rotate the validation fold";
    style="rounded,filled";
    color="#F2C96D";
    fillcolor="#FFFAE8";

    f1 [label="Fold 1\nTrain: other K−1 folds\nValidate: fold 1", fillcolor="#C8E6C9"];
    f2 [label="Fold 2\nTrain: other K−1 folds\nValidate: fold 2", fillcolor="#F7C6C7"];
    fk [label="Fold K\nTrain: other K−1 folds\nValidate: fold K", fillcolor="#C8E6C9"];

    f1 -> f2 [style=dashed, label="next fold"];
    f2 -> fk [style=dashed, label="…"];
  }

  params -> f1 [style=dotted];
  params -> f2 [style=dotted];
  params -> fk [style=dotted];
  f1 -> scores;
  f2 -> scores;
  fk -> scores;
}
```

Save it as `kfold_tuning.dot` and render it with `dot -Tpng kfold_tuning.dot -o kfold_tuning.png`.

**Evaluation:** Assistant A’s answer is better. Its DOT source is substantially valid and gives a clear, colorful high-level flow from data through cross-validation to selection. However, it shows only one hyperparameter set, so it does not fully illustrate comparing candidate settings during tuning. The fold nodes also describe the train/validation split rather than making the rotation of validation folds especially explicit.

Assistant B appears more elaborate, but its DOT source contains syntax errors that prevent rendering as written, including malformed edge statements such as `dataset -> |fold_0| ;` and `legend_note .. legend_note;`. The identifier `fold_{k-1}` is also not a valid unquoted DOT identifier. It further describes a fold’s training set as the “full dataset,” which is inaccurate: it should contain the other \(k-1\) folds. B’s extra rendering guidance and explanation do not make up for code that cannot be rendered.

Both answers would benefit from explicitly showing that each candidate hyperparameter configuration is evaluated across all folds, its scores are aggregated, and the best configuration is selected. A is clearer and more usable despite that omission.

My final verdict is: [[A>>B]]

