Here is a colourful Graphviz DOT schematic for a five-fold hyperparameter-tuning example. Each fold uses a different held-out validation subset; scores are aggregated for a candidate configuration before selecting the best configuration.

```dot
digraph KFoldTuning {
  rankdir=LR;
  graph [fontname="Arial", bgcolor="#FFFDF7", pad=0.25, nodesep=0.35];
  node [fontname="Arial", fontsize=10, style="rounded,filled", penwidth=1.3];
  edge [fontname="Arial", fontsize=9, color="#546E7A", penwidth=1.2];

  data [label="Training dataset", shape=folder,
        fillcolor="#FFF2B2", color="#D4A017"];
  params [label="Candidate hyperparameters\nθ", shape=box,
          fillcolor="#D9F2D9", color="#4C9A62"];

  subgraph cluster_cv {
    label="5-fold cross-validation (repeat for each candidate θ)";
    style="rounded,dashed";
    color="#7AA6C2";
    fontname="Arial";

    f1 [label="Fold 1\nTrain: folds 2–5\nValidate: fold 1", fillcolor="#DCEEFF", color="#3973AC"];
    f2 [label="Fold 2\nTrain: folds 1, 3–5\nValidate: fold 2", fillcolor="#E8E0FF", color="#7353A6"];
    f3 [label="Fold 3\nTrain: folds 1–2, 4–5\nValidate: fold 3", fillcolor="#FFE5D4", color="#C56B2D"];
    f4 [label="Fold 4\nTrain: folds 1–3, 5\nValidate: fold 4", fillcolor="#FCE0E8", color="#B84C70"];
    f5 [label="Fold 5\nTrain: folds 1–4\nValidate: fold 5", fillcolor="#DDF3EE", color="#318575"];

    s1 [label="Score 1", shape=ellipse, fillcolor="#F3F3F3", color="#777777"];
    s2 [label="Score 2", shape=ellipse, fillcolor="#F3F3F3", color="#777777"];
    s3 [label="Score 3", shape=ellipse, fillcolor="#F3F3F3", color="#777777"];
    s4 [label="Score 4", shape=ellipse, fillcolor="#F3F3F3", color="#777777"];
    s5 [label="Score 5", shape=ellipse, fillcolor="#F3F3F3", color="#777777"];

    f1 -> s1;
    f2 -> s2;
    f3 -> s3;
    f4 -> s4;
    f5 -> s5;
  }

  aggregate [label="Aggregate fold scores\n(e.g., mean ± std. dev.)",
             shape=box, fillcolor="#FFF0C9", color="#D99A18"];
  select [label="Select best\nhyperparameters", shape=diamond,
          fillcolor="#E8D9F5", color="#8052A3"];
  refit [label="Refit model on all\ntraining data", shape=box,
         fillcolor="#D6F0E0", color="#37845A"];
  model [label="Tuned model", shape=box, penwidth=2,
         fillcolor="#B8E3C8", color="#26734D"];

  params -> f1;
  params -> f2;
  params -> f3;
  params -> f4;
  params -> f5;

  s1 -> aggregate;
  s2 -> aggregate;
  s3 -> aggregate;
  s4 -> aggregate;
  s5 -> aggregate;

  aggregate -> select;
  select -> refit;
  data -> refit;
  refit -> model;
}
```

Save it as `kfold_tuning.dot` and render it with:

```bash
dot -Tpng kfold_tuning.dot -o kfold_tuning.png
```

### Evaluation

Assistant B’s answer is better.

My answer uses a valid DOT structure and makes the key process explicit: each candidate hyperparameter configuration is evaluated across the folds, the fold scores are aggregated, the best configuration is selected, and the model is refit on all training data.

Assistant A’s answer has major syntax errors that prevent it from rendering as provided. For example, statements such as `dataset -> |fold_0| ;`, `score -> |metric_display| ;`, and `legend_note .. legend_note;` are not valid DOT syntax. The node ID `fold_{k-1}` is also invalid as written. Its note about replacing `fold_{k-1}` does not fix the other syntax issues. In addition, some of its intended process is unclear: it connects the validation set to the model-tuning node as though validation data were used to fit the model.

Assistant B provides a mostly valid, colourful DOT example and straightforward render instructions. Its diagram is less complete than ideal: it shows only one hyperparameter set and does not explicitly show fitting a model or computing a metric in each fold. The diagram sends validation-set nodes to the aggregation node rather than showing fold scores, though its explanation correctly says that performance metrics are aggregated. These are shortcomings, but they are less serious than Assistant A’s invalid DOT syntax. Neither answer needs creative novelty; clarity and renderability are the main priorities for this request.

My final verdict is: [[B>>A]]

